---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import EfficiencyComparison from "../components/EfficiencyComparison.astro";
import BigQueryCostCalculator from "../components/BigQueryCostCalculator.astro";

const operatingModel = [
  ["Append the durable layer", "Transform and insert new activities. Reconcile only the source history that can still change."],
  ["Compile the result on read", "Choose a cohort, append relationships, and project only the time range, activities, and features the question needs."],
  ["Reuse an unchanged result", "Let the warehouse or BI layer serve a valid cache hit instead of recomputing the same answer."],
  ["Materialize proven demand", "Persist a frequently consumed result and rebuild the affected window under an explicit late-data contract."],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Efficiency — Activity Schema"
  description="How append-oriented activity processing, schema on read, partition pruning, caching, and bounded materialization can support fresher analytics without blindly rebuilding every model."
  path="/efficiency/"
>
  <header class="page-hero shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Efficiency</p>
    <h1>Fresh data should not require rebuilding every answer.</h1>
    <p class="lede">A daily model chain can look reasonable until the business asks for 30-minute freshness and one-off AI questions. Activity Schema separates <strong>maintaining reusable events</strong> from <strong>shaping a result when it is consumed.</strong></p>
  </header>

  <section class="section-tight"><div class="shell"><EfficiencyComparison /></div></section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split"><div><p class="eyebrow">The scheduled-work problem</p><h2>Freshness multiplies repeated scans.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>A full-refresh table that runs once per day has 30 scheduled runs in a 30-day month. The same chain running every 30 minutes has 1,440 runs—48 times as many—before anyone opens a report.</p><p>When each derived table depends on another full-scan table, a single freshness promise can propagate through staging, models, marts, and outputs. On byte-scanned warehouses, cost grows with the columns and partitions each query reads, not with a row count alone.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section calculator-section">
    <div class="shell calculator-intro"><p class="eyebrow">An explicit BigQuery scenario</p><h2>Change the assumptions. Watch the economics move.</h2><p class="lede">The default uses 100 million rows, 1 KiB scanned per row, three full-scan layers, 0.5% new data per day, and two uncached reads that each scan 10% of the modeled data.</p></div>
    <div class="shell calculator-wrap"><BigQueryCostCalculator /></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section fair-section">
    <div class="shell split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Be fair to the alternatives</p><h2>Good incremental models and materialized views already reduce work.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>dbt incremental models can process new or changed rows instead of rebuilding a table. BigQuery materialized views use incremental updates where possible and can refresh automatically. Partitioning, clustering, projection, and dry-run estimates can reduce bytes scanned in any design.</p><p>The comparison above targets repeated <em>full-refresh dependency chains</em>, not every dbt project or materialized view. Activity Schema's advantage is architectural: independent event definitions make append processing and query-time assembly the default mental model instead of a model-by-model optimization project.</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/pricing">BigQuery pricing <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/materialized-views-manage">Materialized-view refresh <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/querying-partitioned-tables">Partition pruning <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section operating-section">
    <div class="shell operating-grid">{operatingModel.map(([title, description], index) => <article><span class="mono">0{index + 1}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{description}</p></article>)}</div>
  </section>

  <section class="section cache-section">
    <div class="shell split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Pay when the answer is used</p><h2>Compile on demand, then reuse when the underlying inputs are unchanged.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Logical views defer computation until a consumer reads them. BigQuery does not charge for a valid cached query hit, and many BI tools have their own caching policies. But caches are conditional: BigQuery's result cache can be invalidated when referenced tables change, exact query text differs, streaming inserts are present, or security and nondeterministic functions prevent reuse.</p><p>An advanced Activity Schema implementation can track which activities and definitions a result actually depends on. That makes targeted invalidation possible: a shipping activity change need not invalidate an acquisition result that never referenced it. This is an implementation optimization, not a guarantee of the 2.0 specification.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/cached-results">Read BigQuery's cache rules <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section window-section">
    <div class="shell split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Bounded rebuilding</p><h2>Time makes affected history easier to reason about.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>If source systems can change only within a documented lookback period, an implementation can delete and rebuild the affected activity partitions instead of all history. The safe window must account for late events and the temporal relationships they can alter; “rebuild ten days” is not universally sufficient.</p><p>Measure the actual source arrival distribution, relationship horizon, partition pruning, and downstream cache behavior. Use warehouse dry runs and observed bytes processed before publishing a savings claim.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="schema-on-read/">See Schema on Read <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="button button-secondary" href="https://github.com/ActivitySchema/ActivitySchema/blob/main/implementation.md">Implementation guide</a></div></div></div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: clamp(48px, 8vw, 100px); }
  .calculator-section, .fair-section, .cache-section { background: var(--white); }
  .calculator-intro { max-width: 900px; }
  .calculator-wrap { margin-top: 72px; }
  .source-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 18px; margin-top: 28px; }
  .source-links a, .text-link { color: var(--seafoam-dark); font-size: .84rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .operating-section { background: var(--midnight); }
  .operating-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16); }
  .operating-grid article { min-height: 320px; padding: 24px; border-right: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16); }
  .operating-grid article:last-child { border-right: 0; }
  .operating-grid span { display: block; margin-bottom: 92px; color: var(--seafoam); font-size: .68rem; }
  .operating-grid h3 { color: var(--white); }
  .operating-grid p { margin: 0; color: rgba(255,255,255,.65); }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .operating-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } .operating-grid article:nth-child(2) { border-right: 0; } }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .split, .operating-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .operating-grid article { min-height: 240px; border-right: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16); } .operating-grid article:last-child { border-bottom: 0; } }
</style>
